data: {"type":"response.reasoning_text.done","item_id":"rs_resp_83ee67b888ec4d0582f835bbe3d692af","output_index":0,"text":"\nWe need n divisible by 12, n has exactly 12 divisors. Smallest such n.\n\nn divisible by 12 = 2^2 · 3. So n = 2^a · 3^b · (other primes), a ≥ 2, b ≥ 1.\n\nNumber of divisors: (a+1)(b+1)... = 12.\n\nPossible exponent patterns for 12 divisors: \n- p^11: 12 divisors\n- p^5 q: (5+1)(1+1)=12\n- p^3 q^2: 4·3=12\n- p^2 q r: 3·2·2=12\n\nNeed a≥2, b≥1.\n\nCase p^3 q^2: n = 2^3 · 3^2 = 72. Divisors: 4·3=12. Divisible by 12? 72/12=6 yes. So 72.\nOr 2^2 · 3^3 = 108, larger.\n\nCase p^2 q r: n = 2^2 · 3 · 5 = 60. Divisors 3·2·2 = 12. 60 divisible by 12? 60/12 = 5, yes. So 60.\n\nCheck smaller: candidates must be multiples of 12 with 12 divisors: 12 (divisors: 1,2,3,4,6,12 = 6), 24 (1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24 = 8), 36 (1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18,36 = 9), 48 (1,2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24,48 = 10), 60 → 12 divisors. Yes, 60.\n\nAnswer: 60.\n","sequence_number":140,"content_index":0}

